Heat source model:  An essential component of the micro-scale simulation is capturing the distributed heat in the Heat Affected Zone (HAZ) at the micro-scale [ 99 ]. Many characteristics of the melt pool cannot be determined through experimental methods. Therefore, real-time monitoring of the temperature evolution is essential for accurately assessing these features, which is challenging. Simulation methods can be a good alternative, providing detailed insights into thermal behavior and microstructural evolution in the melt pool [ 100 ]. By understanding the temperature distribution in the melt pool, heat source model or analytical equations can be developed to determine the melt pool dimensions and then be used in a multi-scale simulation to assess residual stresses and distortions. As shown in Fig.  10 , measuring the depth, width, and length of the melt pool enables researchers to determine the accuracy of the heat source models and analytical solutions like Rosenthal’s equation. Rosenthal [ 101 ] developed an analytical solution for the heat transfer in the context of welding, assuming that latent heat of fusion, convection, radiation, and thermo-capillary effects can be neglected during the process. In this model, the laser beam is treated as a point source moving in the x-direction. The resulting temperature distribution is expressed by the following equation: 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
