Inherent Strain (IS) is a computational approach used to evaluate the residual stress in a manufactured component. In this case, the IS obtained from meso-scale simulation is used in macro-scale simulation in quasi-static equilibrium steps, resulting in significantly reducing the cost of computation [ 114 ]. According to the classical IS method, once the fabricated part has cooled to ambient temperature, the thermal strain is fully dissipated, and all residual inelastic strains, except for the plastic strain, can be considered negligible. Consequently, the elastic strain is fully relieved, and the plastic strain can be considered as the IS that retained within the part. This can be defined as follows: 3 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
