residual stresses. However, they noted some discrepancies regarding tensile residual stresses in the deeper sublayers of the printed parts. Also, the Zhang et al. [ 117 ] investigated the effect of varying mesh sizes on the distortion derived from the IS method in the LPBF process of Hastelloy-X material. This study utilized the lump layer method, substituting lump layers for actual layers, which introduces errors in distortion calculations. Mesh sizes ranging from 800 µm to 20 µm were evaluated for cantilever components with a layer thickness of 40 µm, considering three different scanning orientations (0°, 45°, and 90°). The results indicate that the closer the lump layers are to the actual layer thickness of the material in real physics, the smaller the errors obtained from the IS method will be derived. Moreover, Makeen et al. [ 118 ] predicted distortion in the LPBF process of Ti-6Al-4V material using the MIS method taking into account geometric effects related to differences in heights and thicknesses. They also explored geometric compensation techniques for the original CAD design to minimize distortion. The study examined bridge-like and wedge components under constant process parameters with varied height and layer thickness ranges. To support their simulations, they utilized residual stress data from existing literature tailored to their specific process parameters. Ultimately, the pre-distorted design, both simulated and fabricated, demonstrated effective results in reducing distortion in the final component.

Geometrical features:  As demonstrated in Sect.  3.3 , localized heat accumulation significantly increases the susceptibility to defect formation; crucially, the manifestation of these heat-accumulated zones is heavily dictated by the specific geometric features of the part. Toorandaz et al. [ 196 ] used an ML approach to predict the surface roughness of Ti-6Al-4V components fabricated by the LPBF process. Their study specifically targeted the roughness of corners and edges, regions prone to heat accumulation in cubic parts. In this study, 27 different parts were fabricated using 21 unique combinations of process parameters, including laser power, laser speed, and hatch spacing (P (125–175 W), V (1000–1500 mm/s), h (0.06–0.1 mm)), while maintaining a fixed layer thickness. They used a Keyence laser scanning confocal microscope to obtain high-resolution, non-contact surface height data. By stitching multiple scans, they mapped the full 10 × 10 mm surface, then computed the Sa parameter, the average surface roughness relative to the mean plane across the area. They classified the surface roughness of the parts into four distinct groups. Several ML models were employed, including RF, GB, XGBoost, SVM, NN, and LSTM networks. The NN and LSTM models demonstrated the best performance in classifying the surface roughness, while RF and XGBoost excelled in predicting the actual roughness values, particularly when addressing the dataset imbalance. Dejene et al. [ 197 ] also aimed to identify which parameters have the most impact on surface finish and to predict surface roughness from AlSi10Mg using ML. Through a cause-and-effect analysis, four main parameters were selected: laser power, scanning speed, layer thickness, and part orientation (build orientation) (P (325—400 W), V (600–1200 mm/s), Lt (40, 60 um), Part orientation (0 and 45°)). Using the Taguchi method [ 198 ] for experimental design, samples were printed and their surface roughness was measured following ISO standards. The researchers measured the surface roughness of the 3D-printed parts using a contact-based measuring tool called the Mitutoyo Surftest SJ-301. Two key roughness metrics were evaluated: Ra (average roughness) and Rq (root mean square roughness). The authors used two ML models (RF and SVR) to predict surface roughness based on the process inputs where RF outperformed SVR. This showed that RF was more reliable, especially for small datasets. The study also examined how energy input (measured as linear and volumetric energy density) relates to surface roughness. Both very low and very high energy inputs led to rougher surfaces due to issues like incomplete melting or excessive heat causing surface defects such as balling and spattering. In another study, Koo et al. [ 199 ] employed ML to predict downskin surface roughness during initial parameter selection. The authors utilized a physical dataset derived from 79 overhang specimens printed on an EOS M290 system. By measuring the average surface roughness ( \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
